<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.9.3: Vertex Form Given a Vertex and Point</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02482</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.9.3: Vertex Form Given a Vertex and Point</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2ba85e56-02e9-452a-87ce-2a15c32f7fce</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <note id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Access the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b62c9150250bf52d6cc37569213ab516e0090dca">Desmos guide PDF</link> for tips on solving problems with the Desmos graphing calculator.</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00002">For questions 1 - 2, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at (5, 8) and also goes through the point \((-3, 6)\).</para>
      <exercise id="_58610f1c-5083-4788-b112-383c4e6b3229" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\) \(6=a(-3-5)^2+8\) \(6=a(-3-5)^2+8\) \(6=a(-8)^2+8\) \(6=a(-8)^2+8\) \(-2=64a\) \(-2=64a\) \(a=\frac{-2}{64}=\frac{-1}{32}\) \(a=\frac{-2}{64}=\frac{-1}{32}\)</para>
          <para id="para-00005">Nice try! The correct answer is \(\frac{-1}{32}\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q#2-->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Write the equation in vertex form.</para>
          <para id="para-00007">Select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00009">\(y=\frac{-1}{32}(x-5)^2+8\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <para id="para-00010">For questions 3 and 4, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at \((-4, 3)\) and also goes through the point \((2, 5)\).</para>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <exercise id="_3fb8876e-eb1f-4f46-99ef-2345f1eefa1b" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\) \(5=a(2-(-4))^2+3\) \(5=a(2-(-4))^2+3\) \(5=a(6)^2+3\) \(5=a(6)^2+3\) \(2=36a\) \(2=36a\) \(a=\frac{2}{36}=\frac{1}{18}\) \(a=\frac{2}{36}=\frac{1}{18}\)</para>
          <para id="para-00013">Nice try! The correct answer is \(\frac{1}{18}\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q#4-->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00014">Write the equation in vertex form.</para>
          <para id="para-00015">Select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00017">\(y=\frac{1}{18}(x+4)^2+3\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para id="para-00018">For questions 5 - 6, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at \((6, -2)\) and also goes through the point \((3, 1)\).</para>
      <exercise id="_672d6cf9-11c0-41b0-8896-377ea42f89d5" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00019">Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00020">\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\) \(1=a(3-6)^2+(-2)\) \(1=a(3-6)^2+(-2)\) \(1=a(-3)^2-2\) \(1=a(-3)^2-2\) \(3=9a\) \(3=9a\) \(a=\frac{3}{9}=\frac{1}{3}\) \(a=\frac{3}{9}=\frac{1}{3}\)</para>
          <para id="para-00021">Nice try! The correct answer is \(\frac{1}{3}\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00022">Write the equation in vertex form.</para>
          <para id="para-00023">Select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00024">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00025">\(y=\frac{1}{3}(x-6)^2-2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Writing an Quadratic Equation from a Vertex and a Point</title>
      <para id="para-00026">Watch the following video to learn more about writing a quadratic equation from a vertex and a point.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/1IUEVwIjywc?si=lbcWBG5YBlTAOW1k;&amp;rel=0"/>
      </media>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00002">
      <para id="para-00027">Which of the following is the quadratic equation of the quadratic with a vertex of \((4, -2)\) that also goes through the point \((6, 5)\)?</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00004">
        <title>Creating Vertex Form from a Vertex and a Point</title>
        <para id="para-00028">Write the quadratic in vertex form that has a vertex at \((3, 4)\) and goes through the point \((1, 2)\).</para>
        <para id="para-00029">First start with the vertex form of a quadratic function.</para>
        <para id="para-00030">\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)</para>
        <para id="para-00031"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Substitute the vertex in at \((h, k)\). \(y=a(x-3)^2+4\)</para>
        <para id="para-00032"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the other point at \((x, y)\). \(2=a(1-3)^2+4\)</para>
        <para id="para-00033"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for \(a\). \(2=a(1-3)^2+4\) \(2=a(-2)^2+4\) \(2=a(4)+4\) \(2-4=a(4)+4-4\) \(-2=a(4)\) \(-\frac24=\frac{a(4)}{4}\) \(a=-\frac12\)</para>
        <para id="para-00034"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Substitute the value of \(a\) value and the vertex \((h, k)\) into the vertex form. \(y=-\frac12(x-3)^2+4\)</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00003">
          <title>Try It: Creating Vertex Form from a Vertex and a Point</title>
          <!--Q#-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
            <problem id="prob-00007">
              <para id="para-00035">Write the quadratic in vertex form that has a vertex at \((2, 5)\) and goes through the point \((3, 7)\).</para>
              <para id="para-00036">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00007">
              <para id="para-00037">Compare your answer:</para>
              <para id="para-00038">\(y=2(x-2)^2+5\)</para>
              <para id="para-00039">First, start with the vertex form of a quadratic function: \(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)</para>
              <para id="para-00040"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Substitute the vertex at \((h, k)\): \(y=a(x-2)^2+5\)</para>
              <para id="para-00041"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the other point at \((x, y)\): \(7=a(3-2)^2+5\)</para>
              <para id="para-00042"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for \(a\): \(7=a(1)^2+5\) \(7-5=a(1)^2+5-5\) \(a=2\)</para>
              <para id="para-00043"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Substitute the value of \(a\) and the vertex \((h, k)\) into the vertex form: \(y=2(x-2)^2+5\)</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
        <!-- Interaction End -->
      </section>
    </section>
